# round(x, n).
#
# Three defects were fixed here, the last of them long after the first two
# (bug-nilpy-round-ndigits-half-up-and-ignores-negative-ndigits, then
# bug-nilpy-float-repr-not-shortest-roundtrip):
#   1. NEGATIVE n was ignored entirely — `for i := 1 to n` does not run when
#      n < 0, so the scale stayed 1.0 and round(1234.5678, -2) returned 1235.0.
#   2. Ties went half-UP; Python's rule is half-to-EVEN.
#   3. The rounding was done on x*10**n, computed in DOUBLES. That was the
#      structural one, and it is why the last two lines below were divergent
#      for two days:
#
#     round(2.675, 2)   CPython 2.67   pxx was 2.68
#     round(2.665, 2)   CPython 2.67   pxx was 2.66
#
# CPython rounds the EXACT decimal value of the double, not x*10**n:
#     Decimal(2.675) = 2.674999999999999822...   -> below the tie -> 2.67
#     Decimal(2.665) = 2.665000000000000035...   -> above the tie -> 2.67
# Both scale to exactly 267.5 / 266.5, which destroys the deciding information,
# so no tie-break rule on the scaled value could ever be right. pyround_n now
# expands the double exactly (the exact-decimal core in pylib.pas), rounds the
# digit string half-to-even on the exact remainder, and reads it back with the
# correctly-rounded parser. Every expectation in this file is CPython's.

# --- no ndigits: half-to-even, and already correct before the fix ---
print(round(0.5), round(1.5), round(2.5), round(3.5), round(-0.5), round(-1.5))
print(round(1.0), round(-2.5))

# --- ndigits: half-to-even (fixed) ---
print(round(0.125, 2), round(2.5, 0))
print(round(2.345, 2), round(0.135, 2), round(1.005, 2))
print(round(3.14159, 3), round(3.14159, 1))

# --- negative ndigits (fixed: was ignored) ---
print(round(1234.5678, -2), round(1234.5678, -1), round(15678.0, -3))
print(round(-1234.5678, -2))

# --- the exact-value ties: only an exact expansion gets both of these ---
print(round(2.675, 2), round(2.665, 2))

# --- more exact-value cases: each of these is decided by digits BEYOND the
# 17 a %.17g approximation would give, or by a place the scale would erase ---
print(round(9.995, 2), round(0.045, 2), round(0.35, 1), round(99.5, -1))
print(round(0.6, 0), round(0.5, 0), round(-0.4, 0), round(1234.5678, -10))
